We investigate a general class of electrostatic MEMS equations given by

\begin{equation} \begin{cases}
  \label{P} \varphi\big(r,-u'(r)\big) = \lambda\int_0^r\frac{f(s)}{g(u(s))}\,\mathrm{d}s, & r\in(0,1), \\ 
  u(r) is strictly bounded between 0 and 1, & r\in(0,1), \\ 
  the function u at r=1 equals 0, \tag{$P_\lambda$}
  \end{cases} \end{equation} with $\varphi$, $g$, $f$ as some functions on the interval [0,1] and a positive parameter $\lambda$. Our results include existence, regularity, and bounds of a touchdown solution for equation \eqref{P} as well as the pull-in voltage. Furthermore, under specific circumstances, such as when the functions are defined as $\varphi(r,v) = r^\alpha |v|^\beta v$, the equation can be used to determine the asymptotic behavior of the solution near the origin.
